WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.000
In the world of TV police procedurals, Longmire&nbsp;
stands alone. The series follows a small police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.080
department in the middle of Wyoming, where&nbsp;
dirt roads replace city concrete and the law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.240
officers wear cowboy boots. Needless to say,&nbsp;
the story of Longmire is far from ordinary.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.560
Longmire is set in the fictional Absaroka&nbsp;
County, Wyoming. The series, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.160
is mostly filmed in New Mexico.&nbsp;
Specifically it's shot in Santa Fe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.240
Pecos, Los Alamos, and Las Vegas, New Mexico -&nbsp;
not to be confused with the Nevada casino town.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.840
The Valles Caldera around Las&nbsp;
Vegas stands in for many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.000
breathtaking Wyoming exteriors,&nbsp;
including Walt Longmire's cabin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.400
The Las Vegas plaza stands in for the&nbsp;
town and workplace of Longmire and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.880
interiors are also shot in New Mexico, at&nbsp;
a place called Garson Studios in Santa Fe.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:53.680
New Mexico offers a 25-35 percent tax credit,&nbsp;
whereas Wyoming tops out at 15 percent so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.280
no surprise that Longmire shoots down south.&nbsp;
New Mexico also has easier transportation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.920
more airports. The busiest airport in the&nbsp;
whole state of Wyoming is Jackson Hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.120
with only one terminal and nine gates.&nbsp;
Plus its roads run across mountains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.080
rivers and canyons where extreme winter weather&nbsp;
has been known to cause closures as late as June.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.400
Any production shot in wild places will&nbsp;
face challenges. When filming out west,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.920
one of those challenges can be wildfires.&nbsp;
While filming Season 2 in 2013, wildfires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.800
surrounded the set locations. The Thompson&nbsp;
Ridge Fire raged on one side of the preserve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.920
while the Tres Lagunas Fire hemmed them in from&nbsp;
another direction, covering the sky with smoke.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.800
At first producers tried to shift locations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.200
Given the fact that the small area in New Mexico&nbsp;
was full of firefighters and emergency crews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.280
however, producer Chris Donahue said to&nbsp;
the Albuquerque Journal that they halted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.920
everything because they, quote, "didn't want&nbsp;
to be in the way of any of the responders."

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.200
The Arizona-based Granite Mountain&nbsp;
Hotshots were part of the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.440
that eventually put out the fires.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, on June 30th, 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.840
19 of those firefighters died fighting&nbsp;
the Yarnell Hill Fire near Prescott.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.040
The season 2 finale, "Bad Medicine," honored&nbsp;
the deceased hotshots in the credits by saying,

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.400
"Dedicated to the Granite Mountain&nbsp;
Hotshots and all the firefighters who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.160
saved the historic buildings in the&nbsp;
Valles Caldera National Preserve."

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.920
Those heroic firefighters would&nbsp;
end up inspiring their own movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.760
Only the Brave, starring Josh Brolin,&nbsp;
Miles Teller, and Jeff Bridges.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.960
Longmire opened up with strong numbers and its&nbsp;
ratings grew over the first three seasons. Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:37.200
2 rose to about 6 million viewers and season 3&nbsp;
only dropped slightly to 5.6 million viewers.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.160
And yet despite the high ratings, Longmire&nbsp;
got canceled after just three seasons.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:43.620
"Why?"

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.440
It was unexpected considering the fact that season&nbsp;
3 ends in a cliffhanger with a father possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.120
killing his son. But, there was another reason for&nbsp;
the cancellation: Longmire's fans were too old.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.280
A&amp;E said it canceled the hit show&nbsp;
because the average viewer age was 60,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.280
versus the 48-year-olds who made&nbsp;
up A&amp;E's average demographic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.320
So apparently it took A&amp;E 3 seasons to&nbsp;
figure out that a police procedural about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.840
cantankerous old cowboy sheriff who refuses to&nbsp;
get a cellphone might appeal to older viewers.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.960
After Longmire was canceled, Warner&nbsp;
Horizon, the producer of Longmire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.320
began shopping it around. Given the large audience&nbsp;
there was obviously demand, especially due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.680
season 3 cliffhanger. A&amp;E had said that the older&nbsp;
audience was less appealing to advertisers, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.400
Netflix doesn't have to worry about advertisers.&nbsp;
So, it was a perfect match for the series.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.800
Netflix picked up the first three seasons and&nbsp;
announced it would launch new ones. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.280
Netflix canceled the series too, though&nbsp;
the streamer's reasons are still unknown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.480
At least it gave the team behind&nbsp;
Longmire the ability to finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.200
their series with a proper finale in season 6.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:57.120
Jacob Nighthorse is a calculating, slippery and&nbsp;
calm villain throughout the Longmire TV series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.480
Even his rise in the tribal committee can be&nbsp;
traced to a murder early on in the series. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.080
Nighthorse doesn't appear in&nbsp;
any of the Longmire books.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.640
Nighthorse is the most unpredictable&nbsp;
and dynamic character in the TV series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.680
A businessman and casino owner, he's often an&nbsp;
enemy of the Absaroka Sheriff's department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.200
and at times even an enemy of his own tribal&nbsp;
law enforcement. But then, he'll testify on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.520
Walt's behalf or support Cady Longmire's&nbsp;
effort to become a lawyer for the tribe.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.920
The performance in the series by actor A&nbsp;
Martinez is so compelling that his name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:35.760
ended up inspiring a character in the Longmire&nbsp;
novel Depth of Winter, Dr. Adan Martinez.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.640
While the character is a great show of&nbsp;
appreciation to Nighthorse and A Martinez,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:46.000
this new doctor can't compare to Nighthorse&nbsp;
for impact on the series and Longmire's life.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.280
There have been many changes from&nbsp;
the books to the television series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.800
but this one adds texture to&nbsp;
the mythology of Walt Longmire.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.320
Absaroka County doesn't exist in Wyoming.&nbsp;
Craig Johnson, the novelist who created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.760
Absaroka County in his Longmire book series,&nbsp;
settled down in Ucross, Wyoming, after years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.240
traveling around the country. However, Johnson&nbsp;
has said that the basis for the town of Durant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.120
is Buffalo, Wyoming, and Buffalo is the&nbsp;
seat of Johnson County. So it would seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:19.280
Absaroka County is based on the real Johnson&nbsp;
County. But would Walt Longmire stop there?

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.200
No. He'd look at the fact that the Wyoming&nbsp;
counties named after mountain ranges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:29.200
such as Teton and Uinta, have part or all of&nbsp;
that range in it. The Absaroka Mountains run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.840
just east of Yellowstone and the Tetons&nbsp;
and extend down to the Wind River Range.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.760
In the episode called "Population&nbsp;
25," Vic Moretti's husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:42.160
Sean, says they're five hours from Jackson&nbsp;
Hole. The combination of the distance from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.400
Jackson as well as the location of the&nbsp;
range leads to Durant being near Cody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.560
Wyoming, or possibly Thermopolis,&nbsp;
the seat of Hot Springs County.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.800
On a map seen in the sheriff's office, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.880
the location for Durant is right&nbsp;
where the real-world Sheridan is.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.520
Where is this Absaroka then? That's a&nbsp;
mystery fit for Walt Longmire himself.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.200
Buffalo, Wyoming, gets the credit for being&nbsp;
the real-life Durant because Johnson said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.360
the town's layout and buildings served as&nbsp;
inspiration while writing the books. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.160
it makes sense given that the town only had&nbsp;
a county sheriff and his team of deputies.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.000
So, there's a Longmire festival&nbsp;
in Buffalo, Wyoming. Longmire Days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.960
features events inspired by the book and TV series&nbsp;
as well as interactions with the cast and crew.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.160
Some of the highlights include talks&nbsp;
with Robert Taylor and Craig Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.080
a horseback ride with some of the&nbsp;
actors, a softball game, and an auction.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.000
There's even a chance to grab a drink at&nbsp;
the Red Pony and in the past they've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.040
events like a talk from the "Two Henrys" in the&nbsp;
show. That event happened during a Longmire Days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.680
festival when the organizers held a talk with Lou&nbsp;
Diamond Phillips, who plays Henry in the series.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:51.200
"It is a beautiful day at the Red Pony&nbsp;
and continual soiree. This is Henry. "

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.480
Robert Taylor, who plays Sheriff Walt Longmire, is&nbsp;
another Australian actor who excels at playing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:02.000
American tough. Show co-creator John Coveny&nbsp;
said in an interview with AssignmentX that,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.520
"[Their] character combination for Walt was&nbsp;
Harrison Ford, Clint Eastwood, and Steve McQueen."

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.440
Coveny went on to explain that they&nbsp;
found that all in Robert Taylor.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.680
For his part, Taylor said to Front&nbsp;
Row Features the role needed a quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.320
"quietness of soulfulness" that&nbsp;
isn't often seen anymore. He added,

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.360
"You would see it in actors like&nbsp;
Gary Cooper and Clint Eastwood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.560
but I didn't base my character&nbsp;
on any of those guys."

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.280
While Robert Taylor may not have purposely&nbsp;
channeled personality traits from all those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.640
legendary cinematic actors, they seem to fit&nbsp;
him like a pair of well-broken-in cowboy boots.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.840
Robert Taylor has been kicking around&nbsp;
Hollywood for years but his most popular and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.800
longest-running role is playing Sheriff Walt&nbsp;
Longmire. However, the role of Walt Longmire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.840
almost went to Coach Taylor, or more specifically&nbsp;
the actor who played him, Kyle Chandler.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.080
Chandler's turn as the coach on the NBC&nbsp;
show Friday Night Lights brought him a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.520
lot of industry buzz. He went on to other&nbsp;
roles in many acclaimed movies, such as Argo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.520
Zero Dark Thirty, The Wolf of Wall&nbsp;
Street, and Manchester by the Sea.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.440
While Chandler's early career included&nbsp;
a country movie, the actor himself is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.360
bit of a joker and might've brought some&nbsp;
of the levity to the series that Longmire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.600
carries through the book series. As he's&nbsp;
matured, Chandler has also adopted a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.280
of the roguish tough cowboy look that&nbsp;
would've fit the sheriff we all know.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:27.440
Executive producer Hunt Baldwin grew up in&nbsp;
Chicago and worked in advertising for many years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:31.680
His parents moved to Wyoming and that bold&nbsp;
move inspired him to eventually leave his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.000
safe existence and move to Los Angeles&nbsp;
to become a full-time writer. There he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.520
worked hard for many years before&nbsp;
eventually fulfilling his dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.440
working with his writing partner John&nbsp;
Coveny on their series, The Closer.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.080
Baldwin and Coveny are both represented&nbsp;
by the same agent at talent agency CAA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.160
and it was that shared connection&nbsp;
at CAA that gave rise to Longmire.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.840
According to Coveny, they had talked to their&nbsp;
agent over the years about wanting to do a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.800
modern western. Coveny mentioned that he had been&nbsp;
a big fan of Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.640
Their agent then mentioned that CAA&nbsp;
also happened to be representing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:08.560
author named Craig Johnson who'd written a&nbsp;
series of modern day westerns centering on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.720
cowboy sheriff. According to Coveny,&nbsp;
they read The Cold Dish and said,

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:16.240
"About ten pages in we knew it was&nbsp;
something we wanted to develop."

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.000
Baldwin and Coveny's experience working&nbsp;
together on LAPD police procedural The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.520
Closer was a big help when they began developing&nbsp;
Longmire. They still needed an executive producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.720
with experience running a show, though. Luckily,&nbsp;
while at The Closer, they met and worked closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.360
with producer Greer Shephard. So they set up a&nbsp;
meeting with her to discuss this new project.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:42.480
Greer is a TV veteran. She produced her first&nbsp;
series in 1997, Nothing Sacred. One of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.920
first big hits as an executive producer&nbsp;
was Nip/Tuck in 2003. She was the perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:53.360
person to shepherd two first-time creators&nbsp;
through a decidedly unique police procedural.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.160
According to Baldwin and Coveny's interview with&nbsp;
AssignmentX, it took about three years from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:04.400
first sit-down to Longmire eventually premiering&nbsp;
on A&amp;E. It quickly became a relationship made in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:09.280
TV heaven. In fact, Shephard mentioned in a 2012&nbsp;
interview with The Hollywood Reporter that if she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:14.480
could add any writer to her staff, she would,&nbsp;
quote, "clone Hunt Baldwin and John Coveny."

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.280
There are many differences between&nbsp;
Craig Johnson's Longmire books and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:24.800
Baldwin and Shephard's Longmire TV series. And&nbsp;
while the book plots inspire the storylines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.360
the series has created many new stories.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.840
In the books, Absaroka County&nbsp;
only had five murders in 24 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:36.320
yet police procedurals on TV tend to&nbsp;
have lots of murders because it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:41.200
for very compelling television. In Longmire,&nbsp;
there's a new murder almost every episode.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.440
Also in the books, Walt Longmire's wife&nbsp;
isn't murdered, but instead dies of cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.000
His wife's murder in the TV series is a central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:53.040
plot point in the series. But one of the&nbsp;
largest differences is Longmire himself.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:58.800
In the books, Walt Longmire is witty. Many would&nbsp;
say he's wryly humorous. In fact, the books are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:03.840
often praised for their humor. But Robert Taylor&nbsp;
plays Sheriff Walt Longmire straight, almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:10.000
to a fault. The only comedy we see from TV Walt&nbsp;
Longmire is when he makes social or technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.680
faux pas, a humor generated from everybody else's&nbsp;
reactions to his unwillingness to embrace the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:21.120
modern world. In the few moments when Walt says&nbsp;
something funny, he doesn't seem to realize it.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:27.600
There are many peculiarities that make Longmire&nbsp;
and its protagonist so memorable. The fact Walt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:31.200
doesn't have a cellphone, nor does&nbsp;
he ever use gloves at crime scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:37.280
are just two. But one of the most well-noted&nbsp;
ticks is Walt Longmire's devotion to Rainier beer.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:41.680
Rainier is a cheap beer that isn't found&nbsp;
all over, comparable in price to PBR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:46.080
and also owned by Pabst Brewing Company. In&nbsp;
an interview with the Casper Star Tribune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:51.040
Johnson explains he was surprised by how&nbsp;
Longmire has impacted Rainier beer. Johnson said,

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.800
"I figured Walt's not a&nbsp;
blueberry, microbrew kind of guy."

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:58.560
Johnson also charges one very&nbsp;
inexpensive and unique item&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:03.280
for speaking at any and all Wyoming&nbsp;
libraries — a six-pack of Rainier.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:07.920
There's one more connection, though. The Longmire&nbsp;
Historic District in Washington is the location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:12.720
of Mount Rainier National Park. So perhaps that&nbsp;
Longmire-Rainier connection may have influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.160
Johnson in naming his famous sheriff. There are no&nbsp;
confirmed statements that say Walt's last name was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:23.200
inspired by his favorite beer. So this might just&nbsp;
be another mystery for Walt Longmire. But, when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:28.720
comes to tying together supposed coincidences&nbsp;
to suss out the truth, there's nobody better.

